---
/**
 * The phase 2 shell — NOT the real layout.
 *
 * It exists to load the global tier and set the theme before first paint. The
 * grid, masthead, footer and band system are phase 3 and belong to whoever
 * writes the layout mechanics; this file should stay boring.
 *
 * Both stylesheets are imported here rather than in a page, so the global tier
 * loads exactly once for every route. Neither sheet imports the other: Rollup
 * concatenates them into a single emitted asset, so the file split is source
 * organization with no delivery cost.
 */
import {Font} from 'astro:assets'
import {serializeGraph, siteGraph, type GraphNode} from '../lib/linked-data'

import '../styles/tokens.css'
import '../styles/base.css'

interface Props {
  title: string
  description?: string
  /**
   * The page's own JSON-LD nodes, appended to the site graph below.
   *
   * A prop rather than each page emitting its own script, because two scripts are two
   * disconnected graphs: an Article here referring to `#person` by `@id` only resolves
   * if the Person is in the SAME `@graph`. Pages contribute nodes; this layout owns the
   * one graph they all land in.
   */
  jsonLd?: GraphNode[]
}

const {title, description, jsonLd} = Astro.props

/* `Astro.site` is set in astro.config for both modes, so this cannot be null in
   practice — but typing says otherwise, and a graph whose `@id`s silently became
   `undefined#person` would validate as a set of unrelated nodes rather than fail. */
if (!Astro.site) throw new Error('[BaseLayout] `site` is unset in astro.config.mjs')
const graph = serializeGraph([...siteGraph(Astro.site), ...(jsonLd ?? [])])
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />

    {
      /*
      Theme and the `js` flag, both resolved BEFORE anything paints.

      `is:inline` is load-bearing. Without it Astro treats this as a module and
      bundles it, which gives it `defer` semantics — it would run after the
      document is parsed, so the page would paint in the wrong theme and then
      correct itself. That flash is the exact failure the three-block CSS in
      tokens.css was written to avoid, and deferring this script reintroduces it.

      ── `html.js` IS THE SAME MECHANISM, SPENT ON A SECOND JOB ─────────────────

      A synchronous inline script runs while <head> is still parsing, so the class
      is on the root element before <body> exists and before the first paint. Any
      rule keyed on `html.js` is therefore true FROM that paint rather than applied
      as a correction to it — which is the whole point: progressive enhancement
      that changes layout has to be in place before anything is drawn, or it is a
      visible shift instead of an enhancement.

      A visitor without JavaScript never gets the class, so those rules never match
      and they see the un-enhanced page. That is the arrangement the Insights index
      needs to hide cards 25+ before paint while keeping ALL of them visible when
      nothing can reveal them again — a control that cannot work should not exist,
      and neither should content nobody can reach.

      Global rather than per-page because it is about the DOCUMENT, not a feature:
      search lands on every page in phase 4 and wants the same flag.

      Only an explicit choice sets the attribute. Following the system means
      leaving `data-theme` ABSENT, so the `prefers-color-scheme` block applies
      untouched — which is why there is no `else` here.

      try/catch because reading localStorage THROWS outright (not returns null)
      when storage is blocked, e.g. Safari with cross-site tracking prevention
      in some embedded contexts. An exception here would break the whole page.
    */
    }
    <script is:inline>
      /* First, and outside the try: a localStorage failure must not cost the page
         its enhancement flag. */
      document.documentElement.classList.add('js')

      try {
        const t = localStorage.getItem('theme')
        if (t === 'light' || t === 'dark') document.documentElement.dataset.theme = t
      } catch {}
    </script>

    {
      /*
      Each <Font> emits its family's @font-face rules — including the
      metric-matched fallback faces Astro derives from the real font metrics —
      plus the preload links, which carry `crossorigin` automatically. That
      attribute is required even same-origin: the CSS font loader always fetches
      in anonymous CORS mode, so a preload without it does not match and the
      font downloads TWICE. Worth knowing, since nothing here says it any more.

      Preload only what paints above the fold on essentially every page. The
      Noto Serif filter excludes the ITALIC, which serves only <em> and
      captions; Lato takes both weights because 700 sets headings and 400 sets
      nav and masthead chrome. A preload is a mandatory high-priority fetch
      competing with the HTML and CSS, so this list stays short.
    */
    }
    <Font cssVariable="--font-prose" preload={[{style: 'normal'}]} />
    <Font cssVariable="--font-heading" preload />

    <title>{title}</title>
    {description && <meta name="description" content={description} />}

    {
      /*
      ── THE JSON-LD GRAPH ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

      One script, one `@graph`, on every page — Organization, Person and WebSite from
      lib/linked-data.ts, plus whatever nodes the page contributed. See that file for
      why the graph is assembled here rather than per page, and for the two defects in
      the old build that are fixed rather than carried across.

      `set:html` because the content is already a JSON string and must reach the
      document verbatim; Astro's normal interpolation would HTML-escape the quotes and
      produce a script body no parser can read. `serializeGraph` does the escaping that
      actually matters — `<` so nothing can close this element early.
    */
    }
    <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={graph} />

    {
      /* PLACED AFTER <title> AND THE META DESCRIPTION deliberately. Those two are what a
         crawler and a preview card need first, and a graph that grows with every page
         type should not sit between the document's name and its summary. */
    }

    {
      /*
      ── rel=author, FOR MICROFORMATS AUTHORSHIP ──────────────────────────────

      The design carries no byline — a detail page's eyebrow is genre and date, and
      on a single-author site that is right. So an `h-entry` here has no visible
      author to mark up, and an in-entry `p-author h-card` would have to be
      invisible content, which microformats discourages.

      The IndieWeb answer is the authorship algorithm: a consumer that finds no
      `p-author` on the entry looks for a page-level `rel=author`, fetches it, and
      takes the REPRESENTATIVE h-card it finds there. One attribute, no hidden
      markup, no change to the design.

      IT IS INERT UNTIL THE HOMEPAGE CARRIES THAT h-card, which is phase 4 item 3.
      Harmless in the meantime — a consumer follows the link, finds no h-card and
      reports no author, which is where it already was. Nothing consumes authorship
      until syndication, and that is phase 8.

      In <head> rather than on the masthead's identity link, which also points at
      `/`. `rel` there means "this is the site's home"; overloading it with an
      authorship claim would put a metadata concern on a nav element, and the two
      would then have to be changed together. Site-wide because it is true
      site-wide, not just on entries.

      The h-card itself will need an author name and avatar. `settings` has neither
      today — two fields Andy is adding.
    */
    }
    <link rel="author" href="/" />
  </head>
  <body>
    <slot />
  </body>
</html>
